-- allium: 3
--
-- JEBOL: evaluating REBOL values.
--
-- REBOL has no fixed grammar for expressions. A block is a flat sequence
-- of values, and meaning comes from evaluating them left to right: each
-- value either stands for itself or reaches forward and consumes the
-- values after it. A word bound to a function of two arguments takes the
-- next two results; a word bound to anything else takes none. That is why
-- `add 1 add 2 3` is six, and why the same block means something
-- different once a word is rebound.
--
-- This module specifies that walk: what each datatype does when evaluated,
-- how arguments are gathered, and how errors travel back out.
--
-- Rules here are written one per decision point, with the alternatives as
-- branches, rather than one rule per outcome. Where several outcomes are
-- possible from the same state, the order of the branches is the order of
-- precedence, and stating it in one place is what stops two outcomes both
-- claiming to apply.

use "./values.allium" as values

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What went wrong while evaluating. Each maps to an error! of category
-- script, except the arithmetic failures, which carry category math.
enum EvaluationFailure {
    no_value            -- a word whose slot holds UNSET was evaluated
    | not_defined       -- a word with no binding was evaluated
    | need_value        -- a set-word with nothing after it to assign
    | no_arg            -- a call reached the end of the block still short
    | no_op_arg         -- an operator at the head of a series, nothing on its left
    | missing_arg       -- an operator further in, nothing on its left
    | expect_arg        -- an argument of the wrong datatype
    | not_in_context    -- a word an object does not have among its own fields
    | invalid_path      -- a path segment that selects nothing
    | cannot_use        -- an operation this datatype does not support
    | locked_word       -- an assignment to a slot marked protected
    | no_refine         -- a refinement the function does not have
    | too_deep          -- nesting past the evaluation depth limit
    | zero_divide       -- division by zero
    | overflow          -- arithmetic outside the representable range
    | thrown            -- an error! value reached during evaluation
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One walk through one block. Nested evaluations, whether from a paren, a
-- function body or a called DO, each get their own, chained through
-- caller so an error can say where it came from.
entity Evaluation {
    code: values/Value              -- the block! being walked
    context: values/Context
    caller: Evaluation?
    depth: Integer                  -- 1 for a top-level DO

    -- The step that is waiting for this evaluation to produce a value.
    -- Absent for a top-level DO, which nothing is waiting on.
    awaiting_step: EvaluationStep?

    status: walking | completed | raised

    steps: EvaluationStep with evaluation = this
    step_count: steps.count
    raised_steps: steps where status = raised

    any_step_raised: raised_steps.count > 0
    exceeded_depth: depth > config.max_evaluation_depth

    -- The walk has nothing left to do: it blew the depth limit before it
    -- started, a step raised, the block was empty, or the last step ran
    -- off the end of the block.
    walk_complete: exceeded_depth
        or any_step_raised
        or is_empty_block(code)
        or last_step_reached_end(this)

    result: values/Value when status = completed
    error: values/Value when status = raised

    transitions status {
        walking -> completed
        walking -> raised
        terminal: completed, raised
    }
}

-- Taking one value from the block and turning it into a result. A step
-- may use up more than the value it started from: a word bound to a
-- function uses up whatever its arguments were built from.
entity EvaluationStep {
    evaluation: Evaluation
    input: values/Value             -- the value the step started from
    input_position: Integer         -- 1-based index of input within the block

    -- Set when this step exists to supply a value to something else: the
    -- set-word waiting to store it, or the call waiting to receive it as
    -- an argument. A step taken in the ordinary course of the walk has
    -- neither.
    supplies_assignment: EvaluationStep?
    supplies_application: Application?

    -- Set when this step starts from an infix operator and the value to
    -- its left has already been produced. That value is the operator's
    -- first argument, so only the second is gathered from the block.
    infix_left: values/Value?

    -- True when the value after this step is a word bound to an operator.
    -- The walk does not move on in that case: the operator takes what was
    -- just produced and continues from there.
    infix_follows: next_value_is_operator(this)

    status: pending | produced | raised

    result: values/Value when status = produced

    -- How many positions this step used up, counting the value it started
    -- from. A self-evaluating value uses one. A word bound to a function
    -- of two arguments uses one plus whatever its arguments used.
    consumed: Integer when status = produced

    failure: EvaluationFailure when status = raised
    error: values/Value when status = raised

    -- What a word-shaped input resolves to. Read once here so the
    -- dispatch branches do not each repeat the lookup.
    is_bound: input.binding != null
    bound_value: slot_value(input)

    transitions status {
        pending -> produced
        pending -> raised
        terminal: produced, raised
    }
}

-- Calling a function or native. Arguments are gathered by evaluating the
-- positions after the call, left to right, each one a full step of its
-- own.
entity Application {
    step: EvaluationStep
    applied: values/Value           -- the function!, native! or op! called

    -- In the order the path wrote them, which decides the order the
    -- arguments after the call are read in.
    requested_refinements: List<String>

    -- The operator's left operand, already produced before the operator
    -- was reached. Absent for an ordinary prefix call.
    infix_left: values/Value?

    status: gathering | ready | invoked | raised

    arguments: Argument with application = this
    supplied_count: arguments.count
    expected_count: required_argument_count(applied, requested_refinements)

    -- An operator arrives with its first argument already in hand, so it
    -- takes one fewer from the block than its arity suggests.
    pre_supplied_count: if infix_left != null: 1 else: 0
    gathered_needed: expected_count - pre_supplied_count

    -- How many values sit after the call in the block it was reached
    -- from. A call needs at least one value per argument still to gather,
    -- though a value may itself be a call that consumes several.
    values_after_call: block_length(step.evaluation.code) - step.input_position
    enough_values_remain: values_after_call >= gathered_needed

    all_arguments_gathered: supplied_count = gathered_needed
    any_argument_mistyped: arguments.any(argument => not argument.type_accepted)

    -- Nothing further can be gathered: either the block ran out or every
    -- argument is in.
    gathering_resolved: not enough_values_remain or all_arguments_gathered

    -- The call has finished, one way or the other, and has something to
    -- hand back to the step that started it.
    call_settled: status = invoked or status = raised

    result: values/Value when status = invoked
    failure: EvaluationFailure when status = raised
    error: values/Value when status = raised

    transitions status {
        gathering -> ready
        gathering -> raised
        ready -> invoked
        terminal: invoked, raised
    }
}

-- Selecting through a path, one segment at a time. `face/color` looks up
-- face, then selects colour from it. `append/only` looks up append, finds
-- a function, and treats what follows as refinements rather than
-- selectors. Which of those a path is doing is not known until the first
-- segment has been looked up.
entity PathEvaluation {
    step: EvaluationStep
    path: values/Value              -- the path!, set-path! or get-path!
    segment_count: path_segment_count(path)

    -- 1-based index of the next segment to apply. Selection starts at the
    -- second segment, because the first is the word that is looked up
    -- rather than selected with.
    position: Integer

    current: values/Value           -- what has been selected so far

    -- Gathered when current is a function, and kept in the order the
    -- path wrote them. The order is not a detail of the path: it is the
    -- order the arguments that follow are read in.
    refinements: List<String>

    status: selecting | selected | raised

    reached_end: position > segment_count
    selects_a_function: current.is_any_function

    result: values/Value when status = selected
    failure: EvaluationFailure when status = raised
    error: values/Value when status = raised

    transitions status {
        selecting -> selected
        selecting -> raised
        terminal: selected, raised
    }
}

entity Argument {
    application: Application
    parameter_name: String
    ordinal: Integer                -- 1-based, in spec order
    value: values/Value
    type_accepted: Boolean
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- How deep nested evaluation may go before JEBOL raises rather than
    -- exhausting the host stack. R3-Alpha reports a stack overflow; JEBOL
    -- reports it as an ordinary REBOL error so scripts can catch it.
    max_evaluation_depth: Integer = 10_000
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- === Walking a block =======================================

rule EvaluationBegins {
    when: Do(code, context)
    ensures: Evaluation.created(
        code: code,
        context: context,
        depth: 1,
        status: walking
    )
}

rule EvaluationTakesFirstValue {
    when: evaluation: Evaluation.created
    requires: evaluation.status = walking
    requires: not evaluation.exceeded_depth
    requires: not is_empty_block(evaluation.code)
    ensures: EvaluationStep.created(
        evaluation: evaluation,
        input: value_at(evaluation.code, 1),
        input_position: 1,
        status: pending
    )
}

rule EvaluationTakesNextValue {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.status transitions_to produced
    requires: step.evaluation.status = walking
    requires: step.supplies_assignment = null
    requires: step.supplies_application = null
    requires: not step.infix_follows
    requires: step.input_position + step.consumed <= block_length(step.evaluation.code)
    ensures: EvaluationStep.created(
        evaluation: step.evaluation,
        input: value_at(step.evaluation.code, step.input_position + step.consumed),
        input_position: step.input_position + step.consumed,
        status: pending
    )

    @guidance
        -- A step that exists to supply a set-word or a call does not
        -- advance the walk itself. Whatever it supplies reports the
        -- positions it used, and the walk carries on from there.
}

-- The single place an evaluation's outcome is decided. The branches are
-- in precedence order: blowing the depth limit beats everything, a raised
-- step beats a result, and an empty block yields UNSET rather than NONE.
-- UNSET is what a caller gets from a function that returned nothing;
-- NONE is a value somebody chose.
rule EvaluationConcludes {
    when: evaluation: Evaluation.walk_complete
    requires: evaluation.status = walking

    ensures:
        if evaluation.exceeded_depth:
            evaluation.status = raised
            evaluation.error = script_error(too_deep)
        else if evaluation.any_step_raised:
            evaluation.status = raised
            evaluation.error = first_raised_error(evaluation)
        else if evaluation.step_count = 0:
            evaluation.status = completed
            evaluation.result = unset_value()
        else:
            evaluation.status = completed
            evaluation.result = last_step_result(evaluation)

    @guidance
        -- A block's value is its last step's value, because a block is a
        -- sequence of expressions rather than one expression.
}

-- A nested evaluation hands its outcome to the step that was waiting on
-- it. This is how a paren's value reaches the block that contained it.
rule NestedEvaluationReportsToItsStep {
    when: evaluation: Evaluation.walk_complete
    requires: evaluation.awaiting_step != null

    ensures:
        if evaluation.any_step_raised or evaluation.exceeded_depth:
            evaluation.awaiting_step.status = raised
            evaluation.awaiting_step.failure = thrown
            evaluation.awaiting_step.error = evaluation.error
        else:
            evaluation.awaiting_step.status = produced
            evaluation.awaiting_step.result = evaluation.result
            evaluation.awaiting_step.consumed = 1
}

-- === Infix operators =======================================
--
-- REBOL has no operator precedence. Every operator binds equally and
-- expressions run strictly left to right, so `2 + 3 * 4` is 20 and not
-- 14. That is not a simplification of the language; it is the language,
-- and it is what lets code that rewrites expressions leave the terms
-- where it found them.
--
-- The whole of infix is this one rule. When a step produces a value and
-- the next thing in the block is a word bound to an operator, the walk
-- does not move on. The operator takes what was just produced as its
-- first argument and carries on from there. Because that applies to the
-- operator's own result too, chains fall out without further machinery,
-- and because it applies while gathering arguments, `add 1 2 * 3` is 7.

rule InfixOperatorContinuesTheWalk {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.status transitions_to produced
    requires: step.infix_follows

    ensures: EvaluationStep.created(
        evaluation: step.evaluation,
        input: value_at(
            step.evaluation.code,
            step.input_position + step.consumed
        ),
        input_position: step.input_position + step.consumed,
        infix_left: step.result,
        supplies_assignment: step.supplies_assignment,
        supplies_application: step.supplies_application,
        status: pending
    )

    @guidance
        -- The continuing step inherits whatever the produced step was
        -- supplying. Without that, `total: 1 + 2` would store 1 and then
        -- evaluate the addition into nothing, and `add 1 2 * 3` would pass
        -- 2 rather than 6.
}

-- An operator reached with nothing on its left fails, and which failure it
-- is depends on where in the series it was reached.
--
-- At the very head of the series it is `no-op-arg`, which names the operator
-- and says what is wrong. Anywhere further in it is `missing-arg`, which says
-- an argument is missing without naming the operator. Both mean the same
-- thing to a reader; only the reported id and the wording differ.
--
-- This is not a distinction JEBOL chose, and it is worth knowing that it is
-- an artefact rather than a design. The C's guard is
-- `if (DSP <= 0 || index == 0) Trap1(RE_NO_OP_ARG, word);` -- the friendly
-- error fires only when nothing at all has been pushed or the walk is at the
-- head. Reached mid-series the C takes a value off the stack that was never
-- meant for it and the imbalance surfaces later as missing-arg. So
-- `do "<> 0"` says no-op-arg and `do next [1 <> 0]` says missing-arg, on the
-- same mistake.
--
-- Specified because a script catching by id sees the difference, and because
-- an implementation that reported one id everywhere would look tidier and be
-- wrong against every assertion in Rebol's own suite.
rule AnOperatorWithNothingOnItsLeftFails {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.status transitions_to raised
    requires: is_operator(step.bound_value)
    requires: step.infix_left = null

    ensures:
        step.failure =
            if step.input_position <= 1: no_op_arg
            else: missing_arg
}

-- === Dispatch ==============================================

-- Most datatypes evaluate to themselves. Notably this includes block!,
-- which is why code can be passed around as data without running, and
-- function!, which is why a function value sitting in a block is not
-- called until a word or path reaches it.
rule InertValueProducesItself {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.created
    requires: step.status = pending
    requires: step.input.datatype in {
        none, logic, integer, decimal, percent, money, char,
        pair, tuple, time, date,
        string, file, url, email, tag, binary,
        block, datatype, object, refinement, issue, native, function
    }
    ensures:
        step.status = produced
        step.result = step.input
        step.consumed = 1
}

-- A word looks up its binding. What it finds decides whether the step
-- produces a value or reaches forward and calls something.
rule WordEvaluates {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.created
    requires: step.status = pending
    requires: step.input.datatype = word

    ensures:
        if not step.is_bound:
            step.status = raised
            step.failure = not_defined
            step.error = script_error(not_defined)
        else if step.bound_value.datatype = unset:
            step.status = raised
            step.failure = no_value
            step.error = script_error(no_value)
        else if step.bound_value.datatype = op and step.infix_left = null:
            step.status = raised
            step.failure = no_op_arg
            step.error = script_error(no_op_arg)
        else if step.bound_value.is_any_function:
            Application.created(
                step: step,
                applied: step.bound_value,
                infix_left: step.infix_left,
                requested_refinements: {},
                status: gathering
            )
        else:
            step.status = produced
            step.result = step.bound_value
            step.consumed = 1

    @guidance
        -- not_defined and no_value are different mistakes. A word that was
        -- never bound and a word bound to a slot nobody assigned need
        -- different messages, because they need different fixes.
}

-- A get-word fetches without calling. :append gives you the function
-- itself, which is how a function gets passed to another function.
rule GetWordEvaluates {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.created
    requires: step.status = pending
    requires: step.input.datatype = get_word

    ensures:
        if not step.is_bound:
            step.status = raised
            step.failure = not_defined
            step.error = script_error(not_defined)
        else:
            step.status = produced
            step.result = step.bound_value
            step.consumed = 1
}

-- A lit-word evaluates to the plain word, stripping one level of quoting.
-- 'foo produces foo, and that word is not then looked up.
rule LitWordEvaluates {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.created
    requires: step.status = pending
    requires: step.input.datatype = lit_word
    ensures:
        step.status = produced
        step.result = as_plain_word(step.input)
        step.consumed = 1
}

rule LitPathEvaluates {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.created
    requires: step.status = pending
    requires: step.input.datatype = lit_path
    ensures:
        step.status = produced
        step.result = as_plain_path(step.input)
        step.consumed = 1
}

-- A set-word evaluates what follows it and stores the result. A set-word
-- at the end of a block is an error rather than an assignment of UNSET,
-- because writing `x:` and nothing else is a mistake, not an instruction
-- to clear x.
rule SetWordEvaluates {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.created
    requires: step.status = pending
    requires: step.input.datatype = set_word

    ensures:
        if step.input_position >= block_length(step.evaluation.code):
            step.status = raised
            step.failure = need_value
            step.error = script_error(need_value)
        else if not step.is_bound:
            step.status = raised
            step.failure = not_defined
            step.error = script_error(not_defined)
        else if is_protected_slot(step.input):
            step.status = raised
            step.failure = locked_word
            step.error = script_error(locked_word)
        else:
            EvaluationStep.created(
                evaluation: step.evaluation,
                input: value_at(step.evaluation.code, step.input_position + 1),
                input_position: step.input_position + 1,
                supplies_assignment: step,
                status: pending
            )
}

-- The assignment finishes when the value it was waiting for arrives. The
-- assigned value is also what the set-word's own step produces, which is
-- why `a: b: 1` sets both.
rule AssignmentCompletes {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.status transitions_to produced
    requires: step.supplies_assignment != null
    requires: not step.infix_follows

    ensures:
        step.supplies_assignment.status = produced
        step.supplies_assignment.result = step.result
        step.supplies_assignment.consumed = 1 + step.consumed
        slot_for(step.supplies_assignment.input).value = step.result
}

rule AssignmentAbandonedOnRaise {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.status transitions_to raised
    requires: step.supplies_assignment != null

    ensures:
        step.supplies_assignment.status = raised
        step.supplies_assignment.failure = step.failure
        step.supplies_assignment.error = step.error

    @guidance
        -- The slot is left as it was. An assignment whose value raised
        -- must not half-write, or a failed expression would leave the
        -- word holding something nobody computed.
}

-- A paren evaluates its contents immediately and produces the last
-- result. This is the only way to force grouping in REBOL, since there is
-- no operator precedence to override.
rule ParenEvaluates {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.created
    requires: step.status = pending
    requires: step.input.datatype = paren
    ensures: Evaluation.created(
        code: as_block(step.input),
        context: step.evaluation.context,
        caller: step.evaluation,
        awaiting_step: step,
        depth: step.evaluation.depth + 1,
        status: walking
    )
}

-- Reaching an error! value while evaluating raises it. Errors are values
-- and can be held and passed around, but evaluating one is how they get
-- thrown.
rule ErrorValueEvaluates {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.created
    requires: step.status = pending
    requires: step.input.datatype = error
    ensures:
        step.status = raised
        step.failure = thrown
        step.error = step.input
}

-- === Paths =================================================

-- A path segment may be a paren, and then it is evaluated and its value
-- used as the selector. `data/(k)` reaches the field named by whatever K
-- holds, and `objects/(keys/1)` computes the selector from another path.
--
-- This is the only way a path selects something decided at run time. A
-- word segment is a name taken as written, so without the paren form a
-- path can only reach what the source spelled out. Rebol's own library
-- leans on it: five of its files use one, and between them they hold a
-- hundred and seventeen definitions that are unreachable without it.
--
-- The paren is one segment, not a nested path: `a/(1 + 1)` has a length
-- of two, and molds back with the parentheses.
rule AParenSegmentIsEvaluatedForItsSelector {
    when: walk: PathEvaluation.segment_read
    requires: walk.status = null

    ensures:
        walk.status = null
}
--
-- A path selects left to right. The first segment is a word and is looked
-- up; every segment after it is a selector applied to what came before,
-- and is not looked up. That is why `video/title` finds the field called
-- title rather than evaluating a word called title, and it is the same
-- reason a lit-word is needed to SELECT by name from a block.
--
-- A segment can itself be computed. `window/pane/:n/color` in
-- corpus/sources/color-names.r has a get-word third segment, so the
-- selector is whatever n holds at the time.

rule PathEvaluates {
    when: step: EvaluationStep.created
    requires: step.status = pending
    requires: step.input.datatype in {path, get_path, set_path}

    ensures:
        if not is_bound(first_segment(step.input)):
            step.status = raised
            step.failure = not_defined
            step.error = script_error(not_defined)
        else:
            PathEvaluation.created(
                step: step,
                path: step.input,
                position: 2,
                current: slot_value(first_segment(step.input)),
                refinements: {},
                status: selecting
            )
}

-- One segment at a time. A word segment selects by its own spelling, an
-- integer indexes a series, and a get-word or paren segment is evaluated
-- first and the result used as the selector.
rule PathSelectsNextSegment {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.created
    requires: selection.status = selecting
    requires: not selection.reached_end
    requires: not selection.selects_a_function

    let selector = selector_at(selection.path, selection.position)

    ensures:
        if not can_select_from(selection.current, selector):
            selection.status = raised
            selection.failure = invalid_path
            selection.error = script_error(invalid_path)
        else:
            selection.current = select_with(selection.current, selector)
            selection.position = selection.position + 1

    @guidance
        -- Selecting past the end of a series gives NONE, because a series
        -- has positions that simply hold nothing. Selecting a name an
        -- object does not have raises, because an object either has that
        -- field or the code is wrong. can_select_from carries that
        -- difference; see the open question on whether R3-Alpha agrees.
}

-- Once a function has been reached, the remaining segments stop being
-- selectors and start naming refinements. `append/only` is APPEND with
-- its ONLY refinement, not a selection of ONLY from APPEND.
--
-- A refinement the function does not have is an error, not something to
-- ignore. This was found by pinning that `parse/all` raises, which it
-- must because /all was removed: JEBOL looked the refined name up and
-- quietly fell back to the plain function when it was missing, so every
-- misspelled refinement in every script ran as though it had been left
-- off. Silence there is worse than the mistake it hides, because code
-- written against an older REBOL goes on looking like it works.
rule PathGathersRefinement {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.created
    requires: selection.status = selecting
    requires: not selection.reached_end
    requires: selection.selects_a_function

    ensures:
        if not selection.current.has_refinement_named(
                refinement_name_at(selection.path, selection.position)):
            selection.status = raised
            selection.failure = no_refine
        else:
            selection.refinements.add(refinement_name_at(selection.path, selection.position))
            selection.position = selection.position + 1
}

-- The path is finished. What happens next depends on what it landed on: a
-- function is called, anything else is the value. A get-path never calls,
-- which is what makes :obj/handler able to pass a function on.
rule PathCompletes {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.reached_end
    requires: selection.status = selecting

    ensures:
        selection.status = selected
        selection.result = selection.current
}

rule PathProducesForItsStep {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.status transitions_to selected
    requires: not selection.selects_a_function

    ensures:
        selection.step.status = produced
        selection.step.result = selection.result
        selection.step.consumed = 1
}

rule PathAppliesFunction {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.status transitions_to selected
    requires: selection.selects_a_function
    requires: selection.path.datatype = path

    ensures: Application.created(
        step: selection.step,
        applied: selection.result,
        requested_refinements: selection.refinements,
        status: gathering
    )
}

rule GetPathDoesNotApply {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.status transitions_to selected
    requires: selection.selects_a_function
    requires: selection.path.datatype = get_path

    ensures:
        selection.step.status = produced
        selection.step.result = selection.result
        selection.step.consumed = 1
}

rule PathRaisesForItsStep {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.status transitions_to raised

    ensures:
        selection.step.status = raised
        selection.step.failure = selection.failure
        selection.step.error = selection.error
}

-- A set-path assigns through the selection rather than reading it.
-- `b/size/x: 1024` in corpus/sources/clock.r reaches size on b and stores
-- into its x. The value comes from the position after the path, exactly
-- as a set-word takes the value after it.
rule SetPathAssignsFollowingValue {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.status transitions_to selected
    requires: selection.path.datatype = set_path

    ensures: EvaluationStep.created(
        evaluation: selection.step.evaluation,
        input: value_at(
            selection.step.evaluation.code,
            selection.step.input_position + 1
        ),
        input_position: selection.step.input_position + 1,
        supplies_assignment: selection.step,
        status: pending
    )
}

-- A set-path resolves its last segment the same way it resolves every earlier
-- one, rather than demanding a plain word there. A paren is evaluated, a
-- get-word is looked up, and a plain word is taken as the field name -- and
-- because the last segment is where the store lands, which field that is may
-- be computed as readily as any other.
--
-- Evaluating a paren fires whatever is inside it, a throw included. So
-- `catch [foo/(throw "ok" 'bar): 3]` catches "ok": the paren runs, the throw
-- unwinds to the catch, and the store never happens. The path is not
-- malformed, it is computed.
--
-- The value being assigned is computed before any segment of the path is
-- walked. So a paren inside the path sees whatever the assignment's own
-- expression just did.
--
-- `b/(c: 2): c + 1` is the case that pins it. The right side runs first and
-- computes `c + 1` from the OLD c, and only then is the path walked, so the
-- paren's `c: 2` decides which field is written but not what is written into
-- it. Walking the path first inverts both halves: the selector would be
-- chosen before the assignment ran and the value would be computed after,
-- which is a different answer on every path holding a paren with a side
-- effect.
--
-- The C is explicit about the order and does it in two consecutive lines:
-- `index = Do_Next(block, index+1, 0);` then `Do_Path(&word, DS_TOP);`. Every
-- kind of target follows from that one ordering -- an object field, a series
-- position, a map key, a bitset bit, a tuple octet, a pair half -- because
-- the path resolution as a whole is what waits, not the store alone.
rule ASetPathComputesItsValueBeforeWalkingItsPath {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.created
    requires: selection.path.datatype = set_path

    ensures:
        the_value_to_store_is_computed_before(
            any_segment_of(selection.path) is_resolved)
}

-- A protected field refuses a write through a path as a REBOL error, with
-- the same id a protected word gives a set-word: `locked-word`.
--
-- Two failures are easy to confuse here and only one of them is right. A
-- protected object as a CONTAINER refuses a new field with `protected`; a
-- protected SLOT refuses a reassignment with `locked-word`. Writing through
-- a path to a field that already exists is the second, so `o/a: 1` on a
-- protected object is locked-word, exactly as `a: 1` on a protected word is.
--
-- Worth stating as behaviour rather than leaving to the storage layer,
-- because the refusal has to be a value a script can catch. Letting the slot
-- machinery raise it made the failure a host exception, which unwinds past
-- every TRY in the script.
rule WritingThroughAPathToAProtectedFieldIsRefused {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.status transitions_to selected
    requires: selection.path.datatype = set_path
    requires: target_is_an_object(selection)
    requires: the_named_field_is_protected(selection)

    ensures:
        selection.step.status = raised
        selection.step.failure = locked_word
}

-- The mistake this corrects matched only a plain word as a valid last
-- segment, so a paren fell through every branch to invalid-path without ever
-- being evaluated -- a computed field name reported as a broken path.
rule ASetPathsLastSegmentIsResolvedLikeAnyOther {
    when: selection: PathEvaluation.created
    requires: selection.path.datatype = set_path
    requires: selection.status = selecting
    requires: last_segment_of(selection.path).datatype = paren

    ensures:
        the_field_the_store_lands_on(selection.path) =
            value_of_evaluating(last_segment_of(selection.path))
}

-- === Applying a function ===================================

rule ApplicationGathersNextArgument {
    when: application: Application.created
    requires: application.status = gathering
    requires: not application.gathering_resolved
    ensures: EvaluationStep.created(
        evaluation: application.step.evaluation,
        input: value_at(
            application.step.evaluation.code,
            application.step.input_position + 1
        ),
        input_position: application.step.input_position + 1,
        supplies_application: application,
        status: pending
    )

    @guidance
        -- Arguments are gathered one at a time, left to right, each by a
        -- full evaluation step. That is what makes `add 1 add 2 3` six:
        -- the first add's second argument is whatever the second add
        -- produced, not the literal 2.
}

-- Which parameter each gathered value lands in.
--
-- The values before the first refinement's belong to the parameters the
-- function requires, in the order it declares them. What follows belongs
-- to the refinements in the order the path wrote them, and not in the
-- order the function declares them. So `sort/compare/skip s 1 3` gives 1
-- to the comparator and 3 to the record size, and writing the same two
-- refinements the other way round swaps which value is which.
--
-- This surprises people, and the alternative surprises them worse: a
-- caller reading their own call left to right would have to know the
-- function's declaration order to say what they had written.
rule RefinementArgumentsFollowThePath {
    when: application: Application.ready
    requires: application.requested_refinements.count > 1
    ensures: ArgumentBinding(
        parameter: parameter_owned_by(
            application.applied,
            application.requested_refinements[refinement_position]),
        value: application.arguments[
            required_parameter_count(application.applied) + argument_position])
}

-- Every argument a call gathered is checked against the parameter it
-- belongs to, and a refinement's argument is checked exactly as a base
-- argument is. There is one type check, not two: nothing about a parameter
-- belonging to a refinement makes its check looser or later.
--
-- So `func [a [string!] /ref1 b [integer!]]` called as `fce/ref1 "a" ""`
-- refuses the empty string, because b's parameter declared integer! and a
-- string is not one. And it refuses `fce/ref1 1 2` on a, because the base
-- parameter is still checked when a refinement is present -- naming a
-- refinement does not excuse the arguments before it.
--
-- The check is against the parameter each argument belongs to, which is
-- what the refinement arguments following the path gives us: an argument
-- gathered for /ref1 belongs to /ref1's parameter and is checked against
-- that, not against whatever base parameter sits at the same ordinal.
rule EachGatheredArgumentIsCheckedAgainstItsOwnParameter {
    when: argument: Argument.created
    ensures: argument.type_accepted =
        parameter_accepts(
            parameter_named(argument.application.applied, argument.parameter_name),
            argument.value)
}

-- A parameter written with no datatypes beside it accepts every value
-- EXCEPT unset. A parameter that really takes unset has to say `any-type!`.
--
-- The two are easy to conflate and they are not the same set. A bare word in
-- a spec means "any value", and "any value" in REBOL does not include the
-- absence of one: the C gives such a parameter `TS_VALUE`, which is every
-- datatype but unset. `any-type!` is the wider set, and a function declaring
-- it is saying it can be handed nothing at all and will cope.
--
-- Getting this wrong is silent in the direction that matters. Treating a
-- bare parameter as accepting unset lets `f: func [x] [...] f ()` through,
-- and the body then works on a value that is not there -- so the failure
-- surfaces somewhere inside the function rather than at its door, where the
-- caller could act on it. Treating any-type! as if it were bare breaks the
-- functions that exist precisely to handle nothing: IF and EITHER take a
-- condition that may be unset, DO takes a value that may be, and
-- TYPE?/UNSET?/NONE?/ERROR? and every other datatype predicate exist to be
-- asked about one.
--
-- The natives that declare any-type! are the ones whose C spec says so, and
-- they are a short list rather than a convenience: the conditionals IF,
-- EITHER and UNLESS; DO; the control signals RETURN, THROW and BREAK/return;
-- the reflectors TYPE?, TO-VALUE, NOT, ALSO and the datatype and typeset
-- predicates; the value-taking series and map verbs SET, PUT, SELECT, FIND,
-- APPEND, INSERT, CHANGE, POKE and AJOIN/with; MAKE, TO, FORM, MOLD, PRINT
-- and PRIN. Everything else takes a value and refuses nothing.
rule ABareParameterAcceptsAnyValueButUnset {
    when: argument: Argument.created
    let parameter = parameter_named(
        argument.application.applied, argument.parameter_name)

    ensures:
        argument.type_accepted =
            if parameter.accepted_types.count = 0:
                argument.value.datatype != unset
            else:
                argument.value.datatype in parameter.accepted_types
}

-- A call to a function opens a frame that knows which function it belongs
-- to. A call to a closure does not.
--
-- Both build a context for the call's own words; the difference is only
-- whether that context is marked as the function's frame. It is observable
-- through one question: CONTEXT? of a word bound into the call. A function's
-- frame answers the function itself, which is how a body reaches its own
-- SPEC-OF from inside; a closure's frame is a real object, so CONTEXT?
-- answers that object.
--
-- That single difference is the whole of what separates the two datatypes
-- here, and something depends on it: USE builds its scope as a closure's
-- frame, so the words it introduces live in an object a script can reach
-- rather than in a function's private word list.
rule ACallFrameKnowsItsFunctionUnlessItIsAClosure {
    when: application: Application.status transitions_to invoked
    requires: is_a_written_function(application.applied)

    ensures:
        frame_is_marked_as_belonging_to_its_function(the_call_frame_of(application))
            = not is_a_closure(application.applied)
}

-- The single place a call's gathering outcome is decided. Running out of
-- block beats a type error, because a missing argument means the type
-- check never had a value to look at. There is no currying in REBOL, so
-- `add 1` at the end of a block is a mistake rather than a partial call.
rule ApplicationResolvesGathering {
    when: application: Application.gathering_resolved
    requires: application.status = gathering

    ensures:
        if not application.enough_values_remain:
            application.status = raised
            application.failure = no_arg
            application.error = script_error(no_arg)
        else if application.any_argument_mistyped:
            application.status = raised
            application.failure = expect_arg
            application.error = script_error(expect_arg)
        else:
            application.status = ready

    @guidance
        -- A type error names the parameter and the datatype it wanted.
        -- A function of four arguments reporting only "bad argument"
        -- leaves the caller counting values.
}

rule ApplicationInvokes {
    when: application: Application.status transitions_to ready
    ensures:
        application.status = invoked
        application.result = invoke(application)

    @guidance
        -- invoke takes the whole call rather than a function and a list,
        -- because an operator's first argument is on the call and not in
        -- the gathered arguments.
}

rule ApplicationReportsToItsStep {
    when: application: Application.call_settled

    ensures:
        if application.status = raised:
            application.step.status = raised
            application.step.failure = application.failure
            application.step.error = application.error
        else:
            application.step.status = produced
            application.step.result = application.result
            application.step.consumed = 1 + arguments_consumed(application)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A step always moves forward. A step that used up nothing would leave
-- the walk taking the same value again, for ever.
invariant StepsMakeProgress {
    for step in EvaluationSteps:
        step.status = produced implies step.consumed >= 1
}

-- A step cannot use up more than the block holds.
invariant StepsStayWithinTheirBlock {
    for step in EvaluationSteps:
        step.status = produced
            implies step.input_position + step.consumed
                <= block_length(step.evaluation.code) + 1
}

invariant StepPositionsAreOneBased {
    for step in EvaluationSteps:
        step.input_position >= 1
}

-- A step supplies at most one thing. A step that was both a set-word's
-- value and a call's argument would have two claimants for one result.
invariant StepSuppliesAtMostOneThing {
    for step in EvaluationSteps:
        step.supplies_assignment != null implies step.supplies_application = null
}

-- An evaluation or step that raised carries an error! value, not some
-- other datatype dressed up as one. Callers catch errors by datatype.
invariant RaisedEvaluationsCarryErrorValues {
    for evaluation in Evaluations:
        evaluation.status = raised implies evaluation.error.datatype = error
}

invariant RaisedStepsCarryErrorValues {
    for step in EvaluationSteps:
        step.status = raised implies step.error.datatype = error
}

-- A completed evaluation had no raised step. The two outcomes are
-- exclusive, and a completed evaluation holding a raised step would mean
-- an error was silently swallowed.
invariant CompletionExcludesRaisedSteps {
    for evaluation in Evaluations:
        evaluation.status = completed implies not evaluation.any_step_raised
}

-- Nesting is bounded, and each nested evaluation is exactly one deeper
-- than the one that started it.
invariant DepthCountsNesting {
    for evaluation in Evaluations:
        evaluation.caller != null
            implies evaluation.depth = evaluation.caller.depth + 1
}

invariant TopLevelEvaluationsStartAtDepthOne {
    for evaluation in Evaluations:
        evaluation.caller = null implies evaluation.depth = 1
}

-- Only a nested evaluation has something waiting on it. A top-level DO
-- reports to its caller, not to a step.
invariant OnlyNestedEvaluationsAreAwaited {
    for evaluation in Evaluations:
        evaluation.awaiting_step != null implies evaluation.caller != null
}

-- Arguments arrive in spec order with no gaps. A function that received
-- its third argument as its second would type-check against the wrong
-- parameter.
invariant ArgumentOrdinalsAreWithinArity {
    for argument in Arguments:
        argument.ordinal >= 1
            and argument.ordinal <= argument.application.gathered_needed
}

-- An operator takes exactly two arguments and supplies the first itself.
-- Anything else is not an operator, whatever it claims to be.
invariant OperatorsTakeTwoArguments {
    for application in Applications:
        application.applied.datatype = op
            implies application.expected_count = 2
                and application.pre_supplied_count = 1
}

-- Only an operator arrives with an argument already in hand.
invariant OnlyOperatorsArrivePartlyApplied {
    for application in Applications:
        application.infix_left != null
            implies application.applied.datatype = op
}

invariant ArgumentOrdinalsAreUnique {
    for first in Arguments:
        for second in Arguments:
            first != second and first.application = second.application
                implies first.ordinal != second.ordinal
}

-- An invoked call type-checked everything it was given and received all
-- of it. Reaching invoked otherwise would mean a check was skipped.
invariant InvokedApplicationsTypeChecked {
    for application in Applications:
        application.status = invoked implies not application.any_argument_mistyped
}

-- Only the arguments a call actually supplied become Arguments, so only
-- they are checked. A refinement that was not named contributes no
-- argument, which is what keeps an absent refinement's declared type from
-- ever meeting a value.
--
-- This is the whole of why `fce "a"` is accepted where `fce/ref1 "a" ""`
-- is refused. Without the refinement, ref1's parameter takes none and is
-- not lined up against the next value in the block: `fce "a" ""` leaves the
-- second string where it was for the next step to read, rather than
-- checking it against b. A model that gathered an argument for every
-- declared parameter would type-check b against a value nobody meant for
-- it, and would consume a value the call was never entitled to.
invariant OnlyNamedRefinementsContributeAnArgument {
    for argument in Arguments:
        let owner = owning_refinement_of(
            argument.application.applied, argument.parameter_name)
        owner != null
            implies contains(argument.application.requested_refinements, owner)
}

invariant InvokedApplicationsAreFullyApplied {
    for application in Applications:
        application.status = invoked
            implies application.supplied_count = application.gathered_needed
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface Evaluating {
    exposes:
        EvaluationFailure

    provides:
        Do(code, context)

    @guarantee ErrorsAreValuesNotHostExceptions
        -- Every failure a script can cause arrives as a REBOL error!
        -- value the script could catch. No evaluation failure escapes as
        -- a host-language exception, including running out of stack,
        -- which is reported as too_deep.

    @guarantee EvaluationOrderIsLeftToRight
        -- Values are taken from the block in order and arguments are
        -- gathered in order. There is no operator precedence and no
        -- reordering, so the only way to change grouping is a paren.

    @guarantee FailedAssignmentsLeaveSlotsAlone
        -- A word keeps its previous value when the expression assigned to
        -- it raises. There is no state in which a slot holds a partially
        -- computed result.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Resolved: assigning through an unbound set-word raises. The evaluator
-- creates no slots. The REPL adapter extends the user context before
-- evaluating, so a fresh x works at the console and does not work inside
-- a function that was handed a block. The convenience belongs at the
-- edge, and the domain stays strict.

-- Resolved: infix operators are in milestone 1, they have no precedence,
-- and they change the walk rather than sitting on top of it. See
-- InfixOperatorContinuesTheWalk above.

-- Resolved against a real R3 (3.22.1), and it was specified backwards
-- until it was asked. A parameter written :word is the hard one: it takes
-- the argument exactly as written, so `f (add 1 2)` hands the function the
-- paren itself and `f :answer` hands it the get-word. A parameter written
-- 'word is the soft one: it takes the argument as written too, unless the
-- value at the call site is a paren, a get-word or a get-path, each of
-- which means "evaluate this one after all". That is what soft means: the
-- function declares the default and the caller keeps a way out of it.
--
-- The mistake was reading the guide's prose about "a literal argument" and
-- assuming the quote mark was the firmer of the two marks because it looks
-- firmer. It is the other way round, and nothing but running it would have
-- said so.

-- Resolved: an error! returned by a native is an ordinary value and
-- raises only when evaluated, as ErrorValueEvaluates states. R3-Alpha
-- raises immediately, which makes an error! almost impossible to build
-- without setting it off. This is a deliberate divergence.

-- Resolved: path evaluation is specified above. Segments after the first
-- are selectors and are not looked up; once a function is reached the
-- remaining segments name refinements.

-- Confirmed against a real R3 (3.22.1). The asymmetry is real and was
-- asserted correctly from reasoning: blk/9 on a three-item block gives
-- NONE, while obj/unknown raises invalid-path. A series has positions
-- that hold nothing; an object either has the field or the code is wrong.

-- Resolved against a real R3: b/missing/x: 1 raises invalid-path. It does
-- not create the intermediate, so a path can never bring an object into
-- being by being assigned through.

-- Resolved: nesting is bounded twice. The evaluator refuses more than
-- config.max_evaluation_depth nested evaluations, and the reader refuses
-- source nested deeper than its own limit. The reader's is the one that
-- fires in practice, because the evaluator still recurses on the host
-- stack for nested blocks; that is a known gap rather than a design.

open question "Evaluation state lives on the heap, so a running evaluation could in principle be suspended, resumed or moved between threads. Nothing here says whether JEBOL promises any of that, or at what granularity a script can be interrupted. A server that wants to bound how long one script runs needs an answer."
